      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The allergenicity of the ornamental tree Robinia pseudoacacia, or black locust, is unknown."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the prevalence of sensitization to R. pseudoacacia pollen, its possible allergenic cross-reactivity with other common pollens, and the potential implication of pollen panallergens (profilin, polcalcin, and 1,3-beta-glucanase) as a cause of sensitization to R. pseudoacacia pollen."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Skin prick testing with R. pseudoacacia pollen was performed in 149 patients with pollinosis. Nasal challenge with R. pseudoacacia pollen was performed in 10 patients. The prevalence of sensitization to the recombinant forms of profilin (rChe a 2), polcalcin (rChe a 3), and the N-terminal of the 1,3-beta-glucanase (rNtD of Ole e 9) was investigated. Immunoblotting, enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay, and competitive inhibition assays were performed with R. pseudoacacia pollen and recombinant pollen allergens."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Sixty-four patients (43%) had positive skin prick test reactions to R. pseudoacacia pollen. Nasal challenge results were positive in 5 sensitized patients and negative in 4 controls and 1 sensitized patient. The allergenic profile of R. pseudoacacia pollen comprises at least the panallergen profilin, a calcium-binding protein, and a 1,3-beta-glucanase. The prevalence of sensitization to rChe a 2, rChe a 3, and rNtD of Ole e 9 was 60%, 33%, and 87%, respectively, among patients sensitized to R. pseudoacacia pollen. Binding of IgE to R. pseudoacacia extract was completely inhibited by Robinia, Chenopodium, Olea, Cupressus, and Lolium extracts."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The high prevalence of R. pseudoacacia pollen sensitization in patients with pollinosis is likely to be due to cross-sensitization to panallergens (profilin, polcalcin, and 1,3-beta-glucanase) from other common pollens. This phenomenon may lead to a diagnosis of \"allergy mirages.\""
        }
      ]},
